course: language
lesson: 34
title: Mandarin compared with English
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mandarin Chinese is the language with the most native speakers on Earth, and it works very differently from English, which makes it a brilliant puzzle.
explain: Mei says "mā, má, mǎ, mà" and her friend hears "ma, ma, ma, ma"; but to Mei they are four words, mother, hemp, horse and scold, because in Mandarin the rise and fall of your voice (the tone) is part of the word.
explain: Mandarin verbs never change for past or present, nouns usually don't change for plural, and questions often just add a little "ma" at the end; the work is done by small extra words and by the situation.
explain: It is written with thousands of characters instead of an alphabet, so learners use pinyin, Chinese sounds written in Latin letters with tone marks, to help them along.
fact: Mandarin is a tonal language: the same sounds with different pitch patterns make different words; it has four main tones and a neutral tone.
fact: "Mā" (high, flat) means mother, "má" (rising) means hemp, "mǎ" (dipping) means horse and "mà" (falling) means scold.
fact: Mandarin verbs don't change for past or present; time is shown with words like "yesterday" or small particles like "了" (le), so "我吃了" means "I ate".
fact: Chinese writing uses thousands of characters, each usually a syllable with a meaning; about 2,000 to 3,000 are needed to read a newspaper.
fact: Pinyin writes Chinese sounds with Latin letters and tone marks; it helps learners with pronunciation.
fact: Mandarin puts a measure word between a number and a noun: "两本书" (liǎng běn shū), two books, uses "本" for books; English does something similar in "two pieces of paper" or "a loaf of bread".
fact: Mandarin nouns usually don't change for plural: "三只猫" means three cats, and the number shows it; for people, "们" (men) can make a plural, as in "朋友们", friends.
fact: "Gung ho" came into English in the 1940s from a Chinese slogan, "gōnghé", understood as "work together"; in English it now means very, sometimes too, eager.
fact: Mandarin has many four-character idioms called chéngyǔ, like "人山人海", "people mountain people sea", meaning a huge crowd.
fact: Adding "吗" (ma) to the end of a sentence turns it into a yes/no question: "你好吗?" (nǐ hǎo ma?) means "How are you?", literally "you good?".
fact: Some say "long time no see" is a word-for-word copy of Chinese "好久不见"; others link it to a different source, and the origin is still argued about.
fact: Mandarin and Cantonese sound so different that speakers can't understand each other's speech, though they share much of the writing system.
exercise: Mei says "mā", "má", "mǎ" and "mà". Why are they four different words? | Each has a different tone | Each is spelled with different letters | They are four accents of one word | They mean the same in different cities
exercise: What does "mǎ" mean? | Horse | Mother | Hemp | Scold
exercise: How many main tones does Mandarin have? | Four | Two | Ten | Twenty-six
exercise: Lucas asks how "I ate" differs from "I eat" in Mandarin. What's true? | The verb doesn't change; words like "了" show it's done | The verb gets "-ed" added at the end, just like in English | You can't talk about the past in Mandarin | The verb is said louder
exercise: Ellie asks how many letters Mandarin has. What's the answer? | It uses thousands of characters, not an alphabet | 26, exactly like the English alphabet from A to Z | 33, like Ukrainian | 4, one for each tone
exercise: About how many characters do you need to read a newspaper? | About 2,000 to 3,000 | About 26 | About 200 | About 100,000
exercise: What is pinyin? | Chinese sounds written with Latin letters | A kind of thin Chinese noodle eaten at New Year | A Chinese character for "pen" | A tone of voice
exercise: Tom says "two book" in Mandarin. What is missing? | A measure word, like "本" for books | An "s" on the end of book, like English | The word "the" | A question word
exercise: Which English phrase is a bit like a measure word? | A loaf of bread | A red apple | The big book | Quickly ran
exercise: Mia asks how to say "cats". How does "三只猫" show it's more than one cat? | The number shows it | The word "猫" changes | An "s" is added | The tone changes
exercise: Where does "gung ho" come from? | A Chinese slogan understood as "work together" | An American cowboy film from the 1950s about a gold rush | A Japanese battle cry | A French song
exercise: Grace's friend says "人山人海". What does it mean? | A huge crowd | A mountain by the sea | Lots of fish | A long journey
exercise: How do you turn a Mandarin sentence into a yes/no question? | Add "吗" (ma) at the end | Swap the first two words | Say it with a sad face | Add "the" at the start
exercise: Leo hears "long time no see" came from Chinese. What's true? | It's possible, but the origin is still argued | It's proven 100 percent Chinese by old letters | It's proven to be French | It's from Latin
exercise: Can Mandarin and Cantonese speakers understand each other's speech? | No, they sound very different | Yes, they're the same language | Yes, but only when whispering | Only on Sundays
exercise: What are chéngyǔ? | Four-character Chinese idioms | Chinese tones | Chinese measure words | Chinese numbers
test: Your mate goes "tones are just a Chinese accent, innit, doesn't change nothin'." What's true? | Tones change which word you're saying | He's right, tones are just a kind of accent | Tones only show if you're angry | Tones only matter in songs
test: What would happen if you said "mà" (scold) when you meant "mā" (mother)? | You'd say a different word | Nothing, people wouldn't notice | It would mean "father" | It would mean "horse"
test: Your Gen Alpha cousin says "Chinese has no past tense so they can't talk about yesterday, that's crazy, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What's true? | They show time with words like "yesterday" and particles like "了" | He's right, Mandarin speakers can't talk about anything that happened in the past | They use "-ed" like English | They only talk about tomorrow
test: A cartoon shows a kid learning "the Chinese alphabet A to Z". What's wrong? | Chinese uses characters, not an A to Z alphabet | Nothing, Chinese has 26 letters, the same as English | It should be Z to A | Chinese has only 4 letters
test: Your mum, a big gamer back in the day, says "be gung ho, charge in!" What does she mean in English today? | Be very eager, maybe too eager | Be very careful and slow | Work in total silence | Go home now
test: Your uncle from Singapore says "this market very crowded lah, people mountain people sea." ("Lah" is a little word at the end of a sentence in Singapore English.) What does he mean? | It's very crowded | It's by the mountains and sea | It's empty | It's selling fish
test: Your mate thinks "all Chinese people speak the same language." What's true? | China has many languages; Mandarin is the most widely spoken | He's right, there's only one language in China, spoken the same way everywhere | Cantonese is the same as Mandarin | Everyone in China speaks English
test: Oscar wants to ask "you good?" in Mandarin. What does he say? | 你好吗? (nǐ hǎo ma?) | 好久不见 (hǎo jiǔ bú jiàn) | 人山人海 (rén shān rén hǎi) | 两本书 (liǎng běn shū)
test: Your sis says "English has nothing like measure words, they're weird." What's fair? | English has some, like "a piece of paper" and "a loaf of bread" | She's right, English has nothing at all like them, they're only Chinese | English has a measure word for every noun | English uses tones instead
test: Your dad says "long time no see is definitely Chinese, end of." What should you say? | It might be, but experts still argue about it | He's right, it's proven | It's definitely from Australia | It's definitely from a TV show
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Mandarin as a tonal language? | The pitch pattern of a syllable distinguishes one word from another | The pitch of the voice conveys only the emotion of the speaker and never alters meaning | Tones are employed solely in song | Tones are a regional accent of no grammatical consequence
exam: Identify the means by which Mandarin indicates that an action is completed. | Particles such as "了" and words denoting time | The suffix "-ed" appended to the verb | A change in the tone of the noun | The omission of the verb
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the English expression "long time no see". | Its origin is disputed, a Chinese source being one proposal among others | It is proven to derive from Latin | It is proven beyond all doubt to derive solely from a Chinese greeting of the same form | It was coined in the year 2000
exam: Ascertain the function of the particle "吗" (ma) at the end of a Mandarin sentence. | It converts a statement into a yes/no question | It marks the plural of nouns denoting persons and animals | It denotes the past tense | It denotes the feminine gender
